rules read back that MATCH what was sent, on id + status + both day counts:
  0 of 2
  a read-back is compared, never counted. A service that does not implement
  ?lifecycle can answer a GET with 200 and a document that is not a lifecycle
  configuration -- the rehearsal store answers with a plain object listing --
  and counting the children of that document is how a PUT that failed with
  409 was once reported as a policy stored and read back.

the policy's arithmetic against the ages the service reports:
  {"versions_enumerated": 12, "versions_with_parsed_age": 12, "unparsed_timestamps": 0, "oldest_version_age_days": 0, "newest_version_age_days": 0, "as_is": {"kept": 12, "expired_current": 0, "expired_noncurrent": 0}, "if_everything_were_31_days_older": {"kept": 0, "expired_current": 12, "expired_noncurrent": 0}, "policy_days": {"current": 7, "noncurrent": 30}}

  read that as: 12 versions exist now, all between 0 and
  0 day(s) old. A 7-day current / 30-day noncurrent policy applied to
  them as they stand keeps 12 of them. Shift every age 31 days into
  the past -- a month of real backups -- and the same policy keeps
  0. The two differ, which is the property that matters: the policy
  keeps the recent side and drops the aged side, rather than dropping both.

  This is ARITHMETIC ON MEASURED AGES, not a retention observation. Whether
  the service EXECUTES the expiry, on what schedule, and whether its clock is
  the one the policy is evaluated against, are the service's answers to give.

restore after the retention step: exit 0, 70|2485|70
  (source was 70|2485|70). Retention that breaks restore is worse than no
  retention, and this is the check that notices.

